  "hints": [
    "Run it. Which component is red? Everything downstream was scaled, but one thing wasn't.",
    "The gateway is both a capacity limit (10,000 rps for 14,000 offered) and a single point of failure.",
    "Give the API Gateway 2 replicas. That doubles capacity to 20,000 rps and removes the single point of failure for $50 more a month."
  ],
  "scoring": {
    "weights": {
      "performance": 0.4,
      "reliability": 0.35,
      "cost": 0.1,
      "architecture": 0.15
    },
    "latencyTargetMs": 60,
    "costBudget": 1450
  },
  "explanation": [
    "Teams scale the obvious tiers (servers, DB) and forget the shared chokepoints in front of them: gateways, load balancers, NAT gateways, auth services.",
    "Every request crosses the entry tier, so it needs the most headroom and must be redundant. In the cloud, managed gateways scale for you. Self-hosted ones (Kong, Envoy, NGINX) need replicas behind DNS or an L4 load balancer.",
    "A rate limiter is the complement: it protects what's behind the gateway from a single abusive client. It doesn't add capacity for legitimate traffic."
  ]
